+# Retry-credit lifecycle contract
+
+This is an internal contract between the reminder send coordinator and retry-credit persistence boundary. It intentionally does not prescribe HTTP, RPC, language, or ORM syntax.
+
+## `reserve(workspace_id, attempt_key)`
+
+Called before a provider send begins.
+
+| Result | Meaning | Required effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| `reserved(reservation_id)` | The attempt owns one credit. | Atomically decrement available credits and persist `reserved`. |
+| `already_reserved(reservation_id)` | Same logical attempt was already started. | Do not decrement again. |
+| `quota_exhausted` | No credit is available. | Do not call the provider. |
+
+The operation must serialize competing reservations for the same workspace through a row lock or equivalent compare-and-update mechanism.
+
+## `settle(reservation_id, outcome)`
+
+Called exactly around every provider outcome, but safe if delivered repeatedly.
+
+Valid outcomes are `accepted`, `failed`, `timed_out`, and `cancelled`.
+
+| Existing state | Outcome | Resulting state | Credit effect |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `reserved` | `accepted` | `finalized` | None |
+| `reserved` | `failed` / `timed_out` / `cancelled` | `released` | Restore exactly one credit. |
+| `finalized` / `released` | any duplicate callback | unchanged | None |
+
+The coordinator must call `settle(..., failed)` for unexpected provider exceptions after classifying any known timeout or cancellation outcome. A provider adapter may return an outcome, but it may not settle quota itself.
+
+## Invariants
+
+- A provider call may occur only after `reserve` returns `reserved` or `already_reserved` for the same in-flight attempt.
+- Distinct attempts can reserve no more credits than the current workspace balance.
+- A failed, timed-out, or cancelled attempt restores one and only one credit.
+- Callback duplication cannot make a balance negative or restore extra credit.
