+# Implementation Plan: Provider retry quota
+
+**Branch**: `S004-provider-retry` | **Date**: 2026-08-10 | **Spec**: [spec.md](spec.md)  
+**Input**: Story specification from `specs/projects/001-pulseboard/epics/E002-reminders/stories/S004-provider-retry/spec.md`
+
+## Summary
+
+Make each reminder-provider retry consume a workspace retry credit only while the send is in flight. The send coordinator will create or reuse a durable reservation inside one database transaction before calling the provider; it will then finalize the reservation on provider acceptance or release it on failure, timeout, or cancellation. A stable attempt key and terminal reservation states make callbacks idempotent and prevent concurrent sends from driving a workspace balance below zero.
+
+## Technical Approach
+
+Introduce a small retry-credit domain module and persistence repository under a new single-service source layout, because this repository currently contains no application source. Store quota state per workspace and a reservation keyed by the logical send attempt. Acquire the quota lock and create the reservation atomically, failing cleanly when no available credit remains. Keep the provider call outside the database transaction, then settle the same reservation through conditional, terminal state transitions so repeated callbacks do not change quota twice. The send coordinator owns all success, failure, timeout, and cancellation paths; provider adapters report an outcome but never mutate credit state directly.
+
+## Dependencies
+
+- Requires: a transactional relational persistence layer capable of row-level locking or equivalent conditional update; a reminder send coordinator that exposes a stable logical attempt identifier.
+- Provides: an idempotent retry-quota boundary that later provider adapters and retry scheduling can call without implementing their own accounting.
+
+## Testing Strategy
+
+- Unit tests: reservation state transitions; duplicate finalization/release; rejection with no available credit; invalid terminal transitions.
+- Integration tests: concurrent reservation attempts against one workspace quota; accepted provider result; provider failure, timeout, and cancellation; repeated callback delivery.
+- Contract tests: send coordinator outcome contract and retry-credit repository return states.
+
+## Technical Context
+
+**Language/Version**: Not yet selected; establish the repository's implementation language before task execution.  
+**Primary Dependencies**: Transactional relational database driver/ORM and the existing provider client (neither is present in this planning fixture).  
+**Storage**: Relational database with transactions and row-level locks, or an equivalent conditional-update primitive.  
+**Testing**: Repository-native unit and integration test runner, to be selected with the implementation stack.  
+**Target Platform**: Backend reminder delivery worker/service.  
+**Project Type**: Single backend service.  
+**Performance Goals**: Reserve and settle in bounded, short database transactions; do not hold a database transaction across a provider network call.  
+**Constraints**: Never create a negative available balance; a logical send attempt may affect quota at most once per lifecycle transition; failure, timeout, and cancellation must release exactly once.  
+**Scale/Scope**: Per-workspace quota coordination for reminder-provider retry attempts; no UI or new provider integration in this story.
+
+## Constitution Check
+
+No project or epic constitution, domain model, UX strategy, design system, user journey, or wireframes are present in this workspace. This is a non-UI story, so no UI-spec gate applies.
+
+**Product Principles**:
+
+- Retry credits represent only provider attempts that remain in flight or were accepted; failed work must not be charged.
+- Operators receive deterministic quota behavior under concurrent retries.
+
+**Technical Excellence**:
+
+- Code Quality: keep reservation state transitions in one domain boundary rather than scattered across provider adapters.
+- Quality Assurance: prove each terminal outcome and duplicate callback path with unit and integration coverage.
+- Performance: transactions cover only quota/reservation mutation, never the provider request.
+- UX Standards: not applicable; this story has no user interface.
+
+**Brand Alignment**: not applicable; this story has no user-facing copy or design surface.
+
+**Initial Constitution Check: PASS.** The plan has no known constitution violation because no constitution is present; the transaction and idempotency constraints are derived directly from the story specification.
+
